-- title:
--   Theorem 1.8.1 — the internal history has a predictable structure adapted to the flow
-- statement:
--   **Theorem 1.8.1.** Let $N$ be a point process compatible with the flow $\{\theta_t\}$ and
--   let $\{Z_n\}$ be a sequence of marks of $(N,\theta_t)$. The internal history
--   $\{\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t\}$ has a **predictable structure adapted to $\{\theta_t\}$**.
--
--   That is: every $\mathcal{F}_t$-predictable process has the form
--   $$ Z(t,\omega) = v(t,\theta_t\omega) \tag{1.8.1} $$
--   with $v$ jointly measurable and each $v(t,\cdot)$ measurable for the strict past
--   $\mathcal{F}_{0-}$. Stated as the equality of two $\sigma$-fields on $\mathbb{R}\times\Omega$,
--   $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t) = \tau(\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t)$, which is what the proof establishes
--   in its two halves $(\alpha)$ and $(\beta)$.
--
--   **This theorem is why form (1.8.1) may be used as the definition of predictability elsewhere in
--   this series.** Mission IV's PASTA takes that form as its hypothesis; without Theorem 1.8.1 it
--   would be a restriction on which processes PASTA applies to. With it, the form is available for
--   every predictable process over an internal history, so nothing is lost. The book says as much: it
--   "will be the key to a short proof and to a generalization of Papangelou's formula in §1.9".
--
--   The first inclusion is where compatibility of the history with the flow is used: for
--   $X(t,\omega) = H(\omega)\mathbf{1}_{(a,b]}(t)$ with $H$ being $\mathcal{F}_a$-measurable, writing
--   $v(t,\omega) = H(\theta_{-t}\omega)\mathbf{1}_{(a,b]}(t)$ makes $v(t,\cdot)$
--   $\mathcal{F}_{a-t}$-measurable, hence $\mathcal{F}_{0-}$-measurable since $t > a$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The conclusion carries both clauses of the definition on p.57: the internal history is compatible with the flow ($\theta_t\mathcal{F}_s = \mathcal{F}_{s-t}$, written `comap (θ t) (H.F s) = H.F (s + t)`), and $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t) = \tau(\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t)$. The book's own use of the notion (proof of Theorem 1.9.2, p.67: "$\{X(t)\} = \{v\circ\theta_t\}$ is an $\mathcal{F}_t$-predictable process") is both inclusions, which is why the equality is the statement.
-- source:
--   Baccelli & Bremaud, Elements of Queueing Theory: Palm Martingale Calculus and Stochastic Recurrences, 2nd ed., Springer 2003, p. 57, Theorem 1.8.1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PalmQueueing_Palm_PointProcess
import Definitions.Def_PalmQueueing_Palm_StochasticIntensity

/-!
# Theorem 1.8.1: the internal history has a predictable structure adapted to the flow (§1.8, p.57)
-/

namespace PalmQueueing.Ergodic

open MeasureTheory
open PalmQueueing.Palm

variable {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- **Theorem 1.8.1** (p.57). Let `N` be a point process compatible with the flow `{θ_t}` and let
`{Z_n}` be a sequence of marks of `(N, θ_t)`. The internal history `{F_t^{N,Z}}` has a
**predictable structure adapted to `{θ_t}`**.

"Predictable structure adapted to `{θ_t}`" is the property that every `F_t`-predictable process has
the form

`(1.8.1)  Z(t, ω) = v(t, θ_t ω)`,

with `v` jointly measurable and each `v(t, ·)` measurable for the strict past `F_{0−}`. Stated as
the equality of two σ-fields on `ℝ × Ω`, which is exactly what the book's proof establishes in its
two halves: `P(F_t^{N,Z}) ⊂ τ(F_t^{N,Z})` and `τ(F_t^{N,Z}) ⊂ P(F_t^{N,Z})`.

**This theorem is why form (1.8.1) may be used as the definition of predictability elsewhere in
this series.** Mission `03`'s PASTA takes `IsPredictable` — the (1.8.1) form — as its hypothesis;
without Theorem 1.8.1 that would be a restriction on the processes PASTA applies to. With it, the
form is available for every predictable process over an internal history, so nothing is lost. The
book says as much: it "will be the key to a short proof and to a generalization of Papangelou's
formula in §1.9".

The first inclusion is where compatibility of the history with the flow is used: for
`X(t,ω) = H(ω)1_{(a,b]}(t)` with `H` `F_a`-measurable, writing `v(t,ω) = H(θ_{−t}ω)1_{(a,b]}(t)`
gives `v(t, ·)` `F_{a−t}`-measurable, hence `F_{0−}`-measurable since `t > a`.

The conclusion carries both halves
of the definition on p.57: the history is compatible with the flow, `θ_t F_s = F_{s−t}` (written
`comap θ_t F_s = F_{s+t}`), and the predictable σ-field is the one generated by (1.8.2). -/
theorem internal_history_predictable {K : Type*} [MeasurableSpace K]
    (θ : Flow Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω) (hN : N.Compatible θ)
    (Z : ℤ → Ω → K) (hZ : IsMarkSequence θ N Z)
    (H : History Ω) (hint : H.IsInternalOf N Z) :
    (∀ s t : ℝ, MeasurableSpace.comap (θ t) (H.F s) = H.F (s + t)) ∧
      H.predictableSigma = H.shiftSigma θ := by sorry

end PalmQueueing.Ergodic
